"05_newfixed.mst — `ByteArray class >> newFixed:`, the OUT-PARAMETER BUFFER.

 `Behavior>>newFixed:` is Dolphin's primitive 76: allocate an indexable byte
 object on the FIXED heap, so the garbage collector cannot move it while a
 Win32 call is writing into it. Dolphin's own comment draws the distinction —
 objects from primitive 71 'are free to move to assist garbage collection,
 whereas those allocated with primitive 76 are in a traditional fixed heap'.

 The `Utf16String` half of this was already answered when DD9 aliased
 `Utf16String` to `Utf16Buffer` (01_view_overrides.mst), which carries its own
 `newFixed:` taking a CHARACTER count. The BYTE-ARRAY half was never written,
 and five sites in the translated wave use it for API out-parameters:

     DisplayMonitor>>getDpi:      ByteArray newFixed: 4    GetDpiForMonitor
     DisplayMonitor>>scaleFactor  ByteArray newFixed: 4
     Canvas>>plgBlt               ByteArray newFixed: 24   three POINTs
     ImageList>>...               ByteArray newFixed: 8
     TextEdit>>selectionRange     ByteArray newFixed: 8    EM_GETSEL

 Each raised `ByteArray class has no method 'newFixed:'` inside a contained
 handler, so they read as five unrelated failures rather than one missing
 method. `DisplayMonitor>>getDpi:` is the one that mattered for DD12: it is
 reached from `effectiveDpi`, the FIRST statement of `cacheInfo`, so no
 monitor could report its rectangle and every window-positioning path above it
 — `showModal`'s centring included — died before calling GetMonitorInfo.

 WHAT IT ANSWERS HERE. This port has nothing to pin against: image objects
 live in the Dart heap and never travel to Windows, and everything that does
 cross the FFI boundary is an `ExternalMemory` — a real malloc'd block at a
 stable address, which is exactly the guarantee primitive 76 exists to give.
 So `newFixed:` answers external memory, and the pinning semantics hold by
 construction rather than by emulation.

 Not a stand-in with a retirement date; there is nothing to retire to. The one
 visible difference from Dolphin is that the answer is an ExternalMemory
 rather than a ByteArray proper, and every caller in the corpus does one of
 exactly two things with it — reads a field back (`int32AtOffset:`, which
 ExternalMemory answers) or hands it to an API (`asParameter`/`yourAddress`,
 likewise)."

ByteArray extend [
    "Dolphin's argument is the SIZE IN BYTES for a byte class."
    ByteArray class >> newFixed: anInteger [ ^ExternalMemory new: anInteger ]
]
